<http://digistrucmed.org/entity/implantation%20of%20a%20true%20bipolar%20lead> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://digistrucmed.org/SurfaceForm> .
<http://digistrucmed.org/entity-mention/lu_6342abf554836b9d5e4fb95b38d26c5f98a3ab831ae1050feca2b48a189a459c_n0007/e_0070> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://digistrucmed.org/TestOrMeasurementEntity> .
<http://digistrucmed.org/ClinicalValue> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://digistrucmed.org/text-unit/lu_6342abf554836b9d5e4fb95b38d26c5f98a3ab831ae1050feca2b48a189a459c_n0007/tu_0003> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Lead- and connector-block failure\nIncludes lead fracture, lead insulation failure, conductor coil fracture, and loose set screws\nFrequent cause of inappropriate shocks. Lead fracture usually causes high lead impedance (1000–2000 Ω) and outer insulation failure low impedance. Inner insulation failure allows the two conductor coils to communicate. A conductor coil exposed to skeletal muscle may cause inappropriate shock due to oversensing (Figure 3D). Can be revealed by an analysis of intracardiac electrograms and event markers (oversensing, non-physiological short intervals <150 ms). Modern ICDs provide alert features for lead integrity. Remote device monitoring is helpful to identify malfunction early. Reprogramming the ICD is usually not sufficient and replacement or reconnection of the lead is necessary. Insertion of a sensing/pacing lead may be considered" .
<http://digistrucmed.org/entity/low%20impedance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "low impedance" .
<http://digistrucmed.org/normative-assertion/lu_6342abf554836b9d5e4fb95b38d26c5f98a3ab831ae1050feca2b48a189a459c_n0007/na_0008> <http://digistrucmed.org/recommendationStatus> "PRESENT" .
